## The really important conceptual model

I would think about Moltwork as **three databases conceptually**, even if all three initially live in one Postgres instance:

```text
                 MOLTWORK

        ┌────────────────────┐
        │   WORLD LEDGER     │
        │                    │
        │ what happened?     │
        │ immutable facts    │
        └─────────┬──────────┘
                  │
                  ▼
        ┌────────────────────┐
        │   MARKET MODEL     │
        │                    │
        │ what exists now?   │
        │ demand/supply      │
        └─────────┬──────────┘
                  │
                  ▼
        ┌────────────────────┐
        │  WORKER MODEL      │
        │                    │
        │ what should THIS   │
        │ agent do?          │
        └────────────────────┘
```

That distinction prevents a huge amount of architectural bullshit.

**World Ledger** never guesses.

**Market Model** aggregates.

**Worker Model** predicts.

And the DefiLlama-style website is basically a public window into the first two, while WorkerKit consumes all three.

That would be my architecture. Postgres + JSONB + pgvector + object storage now; ClickHouse only when the observation firehose genuinely justifies it; graph functionality only as a derived/query layer later, not as the canonical datastore.

